Chapter: DepthSortSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter DepthSort
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 DepthSort, graded on the two properties its docstring says are load-bearing.

 THE CHAPTER ALREADY KNOWS WHAT MATTERS ABOUT IT and says so at length: the sort
 must be STABLE, and it must DECLINE TO ORDER two depths the game cannot tell
 apart. Both claims have a bug behind them. PORTING_NOTES D9 records a rail post
 and a rail bar swapping places in a 3,091-command stream -- one adjacent
 transposition, every other command identical -- and the tie slack is the fix.

 NEITHER PROPERTY HAD A TEST THAT NAMED IT. The only thing grading this chapter
 is RenderCheck, which builds a whole frame and compares the command stream, so
 a stability regression reaches us as one transposed command a thousand lines
 into a diff -- exactly the form the original bug arrived in, and exactly the
 form that is hardest to read. A property worth a paragraph of prose is worth an
 assertion that fails by name.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM. They are the ORDER ITSELF, worked out from
 the two rules rather than from any implementation: far-to-near by depth, and
 equal depths keep the order they were merged in. Ten items with three separate
 groups of ties give an answer -- [3, 7, 0, 2, 6, 5, 1, 4, 8, 9] -- that a
 correct stable sort must produce and that an UNSTABLE sort of the same list has
 no reason to. That is the point of the ties: without them every stable and
 unstable sort agree, and the file would grade nothing about stability at all.

 THE SLACK IS GRADED FROM BOTH SIDES OF ITS OWN THRESHOLD, which is the only way
 to assert a tolerance rather than merely benefit from one. `deeper-than` is
 `x - y > sort-tie * max (|y|, 1)` with sort-tie 6e-8, so at y = 100 the
 threshold is 6e-6: a pair 4e-6 apart must NOT be reordered and a pair 1e-5
 apart MUST be. A test that only ever checked the first half would pass just as
 well against a sort that never reorders anything.

 We say:

Section: The Items

 Ten items, far-to-near order deliberately scrambled, with three groups sharing
 a depth exactly. Every kind appears, so the sort is shown to carry its tag and
 its index and not merely its depth.

  mixed : List Item
  mixed =
    [ Item { fwd = 30.0, kind = KTree, i = 0 },
      Item { fwd = 10.0, kind = KCow, i = 1 },
      Item { fwd = 30.0, kind = KRail, i = 2 },
      Item { fwd = 50.0, kind = KTower, i = 3 },
      Item { fwd = 10.0, kind = KCat, i = 4 },
      Item { fwd = 20.0, kind = KTruck, i = 5 },
      Item { fwd = 30.0, kind = KTree, i = 6 },
      Item { fwd = 40.0, kind = KRail, i = 7 },
      Item { fwd = 10.0, kind = KCow, i = 8 },
      Item { fwd = 5.0, kind = KCat, i = 9 } ]

Section: Reading An Item Back

 Three projections, because the three things an Item carries fail independently:
 a sort can get the depths right and drop an index, or carry the index and lose
 the tag. The kind is coded to an Integer because `show` on a constructor is not
 something this port asks for, and an Integer index into a failure message
 points at one item.

  fwds : List Item, Integer -> List Real
  fwds (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then []
    else [(list-at xs i).fwd] & fwds xs (i + 1)

  idxs : List Item, Integer -> List Integer
  idxs (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then []
    else [(list-at xs i).i] & idxs xs (i + 1)

  kind-code : Kind -> Integer
  kind-code (k) =
   when k
    is KTree -> 0
    is KTower -> 1
    is KCow -> 2
    is KCat -> 3
    is KTruck -> 4
    is KRail -> 5

  kinds : List Item, Integer -> List Integer
  kinds (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then []
    else [kind-code (list-at xs i).kind] & kinds xs (i + 1)

Section: The Order

 Far to near. Depths are exact literals and the comparison is exact, so this is
 graded at 0.0: a tolerance here would only be somewhere for an error to hide.

  order-want : List Real
  order-want = [50.0, 40.0, 30.0, 30.0, 30.0, 20.0, 10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 5.0]

Section: Stability

 The three tied groups keep merge order: 0 before 2 before 6 at depth 30, and 1
 before 4 before 8 at depth 10. This is the assertion `core/Sort` would fail --
 the foreword's `sort-by` is a median-of-three quicksort, which swaps across
 equal keys and whose chapter claims no stability. The chapter's prose says not
 to substitute it; this line is what would notice if someone did.

  stable-want : List Integer
  stable-want = [3, 7, 0, 2, 6, 5, 1, 4, 8, 9]

  kind-want : List Integer
  kind-want = [1, 5, 0, 5, 0, 4, 2, 3, 2, 3]

Section: The Tie Slack, From Both Sides

 Four two-item lists, each holding the shallower item FIRST so that a reorder is
 visible as [1, 0]. Two straddle the threshold at depth 100, where the relative
 term governs and the gap is 6e-6. Two straddle it at depth 0.5, where the
 max(|y|, 1) floor governs instead and the gap is 6e-8 regardless of how small
 the depths get -- the arm that would go unexercised if every case sat out at
 100, and the arm that keeps the rule from collapsing to a bare relative test
 near zero.

  tie-near : List Item
  tie-near = [Item { fwd = 100.0, kind = KTree, i = 0 }, Item { fwd = 100.000004, kind = KTree, i = 1 }]

  tie-far : List Item
  tie-far = [Item { fwd = 100.0, kind = KTree, i = 0 }, Item { fwd = 100.00001, kind = KTree, i = 1 }]

  zero-near : List Item
  zero-near = [Item { fwd = 0.5, kind = KTree, i = 0 }, Item { fwd = 0.50000004, kind = KTree, i = 1 }]

  zero-far : List Item
  zero-far = [Item { fwd = 0.5, kind = KTree, i = 0 }, Item { fwd = 0.5000001, kind = KTree, i = 1 }]

  slack-got : List Integer
  slack-got = (idxs (sort-items tie-near) 0) & (idxs (sort-items tie-far) 0) & (idxs (sort-items zero-near) 0) & (idxs (sort-items zero-far) 0)

  slack-want : List Integer
  slack-want = [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "d-order " (fwds (sort-items mixed) 0) order-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "d-stable" (idxs (sort-items mixed) 0) stable-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "d-kind  " (kinds (sort-items mixed) 0) kind-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "d-slack " slack-got slack-want)
  end
